Your own periodic jobs, one per line, run by your `timers` user service from
your first login to your last.

    NAME  TIMESPEC...  --  COMMAND...

The shape is /etc/kdos/timers.d's, and TIMESPEC is `snooze`'s own options —
`-H 9 -M 0` for nine in the morning, `-d /2` for every second day. `snooze(1)`
is the whole reference; nothing is invented here. There is no `--help`.

`-s SLACK` IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. A snooze that has just started begins
looking one second from now, so a slot already past is simply the same slot
next time round, whatever the slack says. `-t FILE` is what changes that: it
starts looking from that file's modification time, and the slack then covers
the gap. The file must be one the job's own program writes after every run:
a file nothing touches, such as a .timer table, leaves each restart of the
loop finding the same slot inside the slack and running the job again until
the slack has passed. Write its path out in full, since a row never goes
through a shell and `~` or `$HOME` reach snooze as those characters. With the
job writing /home/you/.cache/mine.stamp, `-s 4h -t /home/you/.cache/mine.stamp`
means "if the machine was off at the slot, run it once when I log in, up to
four hours late".

THE COMMAND IS AN ARGUMENT VECTOR. No pipe, no redirection, no `&&`: a program
that writes a file takes a flag naming it. That is what lets this file be
parsed instead of sourced, and it is why a line here cannot run something you
did not write.

EVERY SLOT WHILE YOU ARE LOGGED IN. `snooze` waits for its slot, runs the
command once and exits, so /usr/local/lib/kdos/user-timers runs each row in a
loop that starts it again: a job here fires at every occurrence of its slot,
not once per login. It is the `timers` service of your own services
(/etc/kdos/user-services, `service --user status timers`), which start at your
first login of any kind — the desktop, a console, either — and stop at your
last, unless you linger (`loginctl enable-linger`), and then they run with no
login at all. A file added or edited while you are logged in is read at the
next start, or now with `service --user restart timers`. A job's error output
is in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/ksvc/kdos-svc.timers.log and in syslog. Use
/etc/kdos/timers.d for a job that must run whether or not anybody is logged in.

A COMMAND HERE CANNOT TAKE AN ARGUMENT WITH A SPACE IN IT. The row is split
into words and never quoted, so `-- kdos notify "Good morning"` reaches the
command as `"Good` and `morning"`. A program that needs a sentence reads it
from a file of its own; `kdos remind` writes its text as this file's first
comment line for exactly that reason.

An example, commented out because a table with a job in it is a decision:

  # weather  -H 7 -M 30 -s 2h  --  kdos notify --time
